{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789288479,"nonce":"pVUKiV3xjfOGQd6+dhVsdakjGHc=","message":"Redefined 2026-09-13 after the owner's review of the first crossing: the\nbridge is the runtime's internal mechanism, never a developer surface.\nThere is no caribou.Script; anyone could wire that with hxcpp. The\ndeliverable of this issue is conventional imports in both directions with\nnothing written by hand:\n\n    // wren\n    import \"haxe:game.Player\" for Player\n    var p = Player.new()\n    p.hit(30)\n\n    // haxe\n    import wren.hud.Hud;\n    var hud = new Hud(player);\n    hud.draw();\n\nMechanism:\n1. Adapters publish loaded modules' interfaces into the core registry:\n   ash from the type table in the .hl (class name, fields with types,\n   protos with signatures, precise); wren_lift from its class layouts,\n   method tables and a declaration file when present.\n2. Each adapter answers the other language's imports from the registry.\n   wren_lift: its resolve_module_fn / load_module_fn / bind_foreign_class_fn\n   / bind_foreign_method_fn callbacks (all exist) resolve a \"haxe:\" module\n   into a synthesised foreign class whose constructor, getters, setters and\n   methods bind to the bridge. ash: its native resolver hands natives of a\n   caribou library to the ash adapter, which binds each to a bridge call on\n   the target object; a Haxe throw inside aborts the Wren fiber and a Wren\n   abort inside throws in Haxe, through the protected boundary already\n   built.\n3. Haxe is static, so it needs the extern at compile time: a caribou build\n   macro (one hxml line) resolves any import under a registered language\n   prefix by asking the core for the module's interface and generating the\n   extern in memory, typed from the declaration file where one exists,\n   Dynamic otherwise. Wren needs no equivalent; its editor completion comes\n   later from the same interface data via the LSP.\n4. Objects passed either way are held by proxies with handle rooting\n   (0268f40), so a Wren-held Haxe object and a Haxe-held Wren object live\n   as long as their holder.\n\nOrder of work: wren importing haxe first (no compiler involvement), then\nthe ash-side native binding, then the build macro. Gate: the two snippets\nabove run in a world with both adapters, and the counter test becomes\nthose imports with no Rust in it.","files":null}]}